# r, the remainder vector, with its sign derived and its exposure measured. One row per layer that
# states both a demand and a nameplate and has a legitimate remainder. The key is (filing, layer) and every downstream fit
# question reads this relation.
#
# It chooses between two arithmetics
#   The choice is the whole file. On a layer built from parts, `r` is `F Φ r_parts - e_n + e_d`,
#   each part's own remainder converted once, so a shared conversion factor appears once per term.
#   Everywhere else `r` is `n - d` from the two filed totals with the bounds crossed. The two agree
#   on every layer whose factors are point values, which is all but the composed layers carrying
#   a factor with spread, and there the crossed difference counts that factor's spread twice and
#   comes out wider.
#
# The crossed difference is a bound there
#   So the crossed difference is a bound on those layers and not their remainder, and taking it as
#   the remainder makes `derived_fit` read `transition` on a filing that is correctly `clearance`.
#   checks/fit_disagrees compares the filed sign against `derived_fit`, so the flagship rule is
#   what would have reported a document that is right.

# And where the pivot is lifted there is no remainder at all
#   On those layers the pivot is the only legitimate figure, so where the composed remainder is
#   lifted the row is dropped rather than filled with the crossed difference;
#   composition/figureless_remainders.sqlc names them. `asrt:Part` says the
#   converted remainder is "never re-derived", and a fallback to `n - d` there hands every rule
#   below a figure that accuses a correct stated quantity. arithmetic/remainder.sqlc gives the
#   dropped layer its row and its reason, and algebra/remainder_in_force.sqlc counts the drop.
#
# Recomputed from whichever `r` is in force
#   `derived_fit` and `exposure` are recomputed from whichever `r` is in force, never carried
#   across from the other. `exposure` is `greatest(-r_low, 0)`, which is `d_high - n_low` on the
#   differenced reading and generalises to the pivoted one without a second expression.
#
# `derived_fit` compares two ranges
#   It is a comparison of two ranges and not of two points: ISO 286's own criterion, borrowed
#   intact. `clearance` = supply exceeds demand across the whole range. `interference` = demand
#   exceeds supply across the whole range. `transition` = the ranges overlap, so which one wins
#   depends on where in the range you land. That third case is the one a two-valued encoding
#   cannot name, and the corpus contains it.
#
# The arm order is a decision
#   The arm order in `derived_fit` is a decision and not an artefact. The three criteria are
#   disjoint everywhere except at one pair, a point nameplate equal to a point demand, where
#   `clearance` and `interference` both hold: chaining them forces
#   `n_low >= d_high >= d_low >= n_high >= n_low`, so every bound is equal and `r = [0, 0, 0]`.
#   The first arm wins and returns `clearance`. ISO 286 counts line-to-line, minimum clearance
#   zero, as a clearance fit, and this pair meets its interference clause too, so the choice is
#   this model's, extending ISO's answer. It is also the only reading whose consequence is right:
#   nothing went unmet, so checks/clearance_with_unserved rules out the two holders that cannot
#   be there. ⚠️ Swapping these two arms would silently reclassify that pair,
#   and `Fit`'s annotation says so in prose rather than leaving it here.
#
# What `exposure` is
#   `exposure` is what the filing's own numbers say could have gone unserved: the largest demand
#   against the smallest supply, floored at zero. It is not a measurement anybody made; it is the
#   ceiling their two measurements imply, which is why the rule built on it compares against what
#   they admit went unserved rather than against a reading.
#
# The magnitude `|r|`
#   `m_low`, `m_mode`, `m_high` are `|r|` as a three-point claim, computed once here so that every
#   rule comparing against `|nameplate - demand|` reads the same figure:
#   checks/shares_do_not_sum and checks/stated_quantity_is_not_the_magnitude. `abs` is not
#   monotone across zero, so where `r` straddles zero the low bound is 0 and the high bound is the
#   larger of `|r_low|` and `|r_high|`; elsewhere the two bounds are those absolute values in order.
#   Proof: `src/proofs/README.md`, entries `crossed_subtraction`, `magnitude` and `exposure`.
#
# One `r`, then everything read from it
#   The inner query picks the `r` in force; the outer one derives the fit, the exposure and the
#   magnitude from it, so none of the three can read a different `r` from the others.
-- layers/differenced_remainder.sqlc, overridden by composition/fused_remainders.sqlc where a
-- composed layer owes an exact remainder, less composition/figureless_remainders.sqlc.
SELECT x.filing, x.layer,
       x.sign, x.sign_absent,
       x.absorber_taxonomy, x.absorber_value, x.absorber_absent,
       x.d_low, x.d_mode, x.d_high, x.unit,
       x.n_low, x.n_mode, x.n_high, x.amount_unit,
       x.r_low, x.r_mode, x.r_high,
       CASE WHEN x.r_low  >= 0 THEN 'clearance'::pm.fit
            WHEN x.r_high <= 0 THEN 'interference'::pm.fit
            ELSE 'transition'::pm.fit END AS derived_fit,
       greatest(-x.r_low, 0) AS exposure,
       x.lumpy, x.quantum_mode, x.quantum_unit,
       x.pivoted,
       CASE WHEN x.r_low <= 0 AND x.r_high >= 0 THEN 0
            ELSE least(abs(x.r_low), abs(x.r_high)) END AS m_low,
       abs(x.r_mode)                                    AS m_mode,
       greatest(abs(x.r_low), abs(x.r_high))            AS m_high
FROM (
    SELECT b.filing, b.layer,
           b.sign, b.sign_absent,
           b.absorber_taxonomy, b.absorber_value, b.absorber_absent,
           b.d_low, b.d_mode, b.d_high, b.unit,
           b.n_low, b.n_mode, b.n_high, b.amount_unit,
           coalesce(f.pivoted_low,  b.r_low)  AS r_low,
           coalesce(f.pivoted_mode, b.r_mode) AS r_mode,
           coalesce(f.pivoted_high, b.r_high) AS r_high,
           b.lumpy, b.quantum_mode, b.quantum_unit,
           (f.pivoted_low IS NOT NULL) AS pivoted
    FROM      (
        :compose(layers/differenced_remainder.sqlc)
    ) b
    LEFT JOIN (
        :compose(composition/fused_remainders.sqlc)
    ) f ON f.composition = b.filing AND f.composed_layer = b.layer
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM ( :compose(composition/figureless_remainders.sqlc) ) g
                      WHERE g.filing = b.filing AND g.layer = b.layer)
) x
